**Convention 2.3 (the credited exact cover consumer).** A nonempty
$H\subseteq S_D$ is admissible if, for every $q\in W_D$, the factor
$\Gamma_q$ on $H$ is constant or a function of one reply $r_x(a)$,
where $a\in W_D$. The witness may depend on $H,q$ and may be
off-target. Equivalently, with a constant experiment $\bot$,

$$
\forall q\in W_D\ \exists a\in W_D\cup\{\bot\}\quad
\forall x,y\in H,\quad
r_x(a)=r_y(a)\Longrightarrow\Gamma_q(x)=\Gamma_q(y).
\tag{PPF.11}
$$

For $a=\bot$, $r_x(a)$ is one common formal symbol, modeling zero
Reads rather than a fifth actual reply. FMR Theorems 2.3 and 2.5
identify $M_1(D)$ with the minimum number of
these sets covering $S_D$. The whole-encoder normalization retains all
original competitors, arbitrary correlated fibers and arbitrary native
late words. It does not independently recolor children or move queries
onto target prefixes. A minimum cover, its order and all its witnesses
can be generated by finite effective enumeration from public $D$.
CBN Theorem 7.1 permits constructor-pair closed cover members without
changing this minimum. Its actual acquisition bound, credited to FMR,
is $2^D-1$ Reads, peak length $D$ and $(D-1)2^D+1$ letters. Those
are sufficient scan costs, rather than preparation optima.

